    cadrumo.application.diagnostics_run_health -> cadrumo.adapters.outbound.llm.run_telemetry
    # The ledger draft path reaches the gated local-inference subpackage for a
    # scan-only document. Deferred inside the function, and pinned here for the
    # same reason the outbound.llm edges above are: cadrumo.llm sits at the
    # ADAPTER tier, so application -> llm is an outward edge. The inward half
    # (llm -> application.ledger, for the draft shape it produces) needs no pin,
    # being a legal inner reach, and resolves through the ledger's public facade.
    # The pins are keyed at the promoted public module names and at the narrow
    # llm target each one actually reaches, rather than at the bare package: the
    # gated readers, the rasteriser and the proposer are separate decisions and a
    # new reach into a different llm module should have to state itself.
    cadrumo.application.ledger.invoice_draft_extraction -> cadrumo.llm.errors
    cadrumo.application.ledger.invoice_draft_extraction -> cadrumo.llm.evidence_draft_text
    cadrumo.application.ledger.invoice_draft_extraction -> cadrumo.llm.evidence_draft_vision
    cadrumo.application.ledger.invoice_draft_extraction -> cadrumo.llm.providers.local
    cadrumo.application.ledger.invoice_draft_extraction -> cadrumo.llm.supply_nature_proposal
    cadrumo.application.ledger.llm_classification -> cadrumo.llm.errors
    cadrumo.application.ledger.llm_classification -> cadrumo.llm.providers.local
    cadrumo.application.ledger.llm_classification -> cadrumo.llm.text_classifier
    cadrumo.application.ledger.llm_classification -> cadrumo.llm.vision_classifier
    # The interchange DTOs are canonically owned by cadrumo.llm, so every
    # ledger consumer of them is an application -> adapter-tier edge. Pinned
    # per origin rather than by widening the contract, so a NEW production
    # edge that is not a DTO consumer still fails loudly.
    cadrumo.application.ledger.invoice_draft_extraction -> cadrumo.llm.models
    cadrumo.application.ledger.llm_classification -> cadrumo.llm.models
    cadrumo.application.ledger.llm_classification -> cadrumo.llm.suggestions
    cadrumo.application.ledger.llm_diagnostics -> cadrumo.llm.models
    cadrumo.application.ledger.llm_review_workflow -> cadrumo.llm.suggestions
    # The tabular fallback lane reaches the gated subpackage for the one
    # judgement it cannot make deterministically: what a file's own header
    # vocabulary means. Deferred inside the resolver, and pinned here because
    # cadrumo.llm is a SIBLING of cadrumo.adapters on the adapter tier rather
    # than an inner one, so this is a peer edge and not an inward reach. Values
    # never cross it -- only headers out, roles back.
    cadrumo.adapters.inbound.financial.providers._mapped_tabular -> cadrumo.llm.column_role_mapping
    # ── aeat-roundtrip-discipline carve-out, cadrumo.llm half (test-file-only) ──
    # cadrumo.llm is enrolled as a PEER of cadrumo.adapters on the adapter tier
    # (`cadrumo.adapters : cadrumo.llm` above), so an application test reaching
    # it is categorically the same edge as the application test reaching
    # adapters that the wildcard two lines up already covers. It was left as a
    # per-test-file list only because the carve-out sweep split on the target
    # package name rather than on the tier, and that list then reproduced
    # exactly the scaling failure `2026-07-08-importlinter-test-carveout-adr`
    # rejected: eleven near-duplicate lines, and two new seam tests
    # (test_invoice_extraction_authority, test_absent_identity_is_not_a_failed_role)
    # broke the contract purely by existing. These are SEAM tests -- they assert
    # the ledger router consumes llm-owned surfaces correctly, so they belong to
    # neither side and cannot be relocated into either without looking like a
    # unit test of something that package does not own. The mirror image is
    # already enrolled by wildcard-equivalent per-origin pins above
    # (llm.tests.test_llm_vision_classifier -> application.ledger._llm_classification),
    # and sanctioning one direction of a seam while refusing the other would be
    # arbitrary. Every one reaches the PUBLIC facade, so what is exempted is
    # only the layer direction, not a private reach. Production
    # application -> llm coupling stays individually pinned above and a NEW
    # production edge still fails loudly.
    cadrumo.application.**.tests.** -> cadrumo.llm.**
    cadrumo.application.evidence.service -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.**
    # The confirmation record is the third of that same family and is pinned for
    # the same reason, not as a convenience: it declares its own secure-object
    # namespace and subclasses SecureBoundRepository, so it IS a ledger store
    # rather than a module reaching through to one. Inversion behind a
    # repository protocol is therefore not available: fifteen application-layer
    # SecureBoundRepository subclasses exist, four of them siblings in
    # application/ledger alone, so relocating this one would make it the
    # outlier rather than the model.
    #
    # Reaching encrypted persistence from the application layer is the ambient
    # design here, not erosion: ~129 production application modules import
    # adapters.persistence (rg -l over src/cadrumo/application, excluding tests
    # and conftest). The count is given with its measurement because an
    # unreproducible figure in a rationale rots into folklore.
    #
    # The family is now spelled at the modules that carry it. Confirmation moved
    # into invoice_confirmation when evidence_draft was split along its seams,
    # and the encrypted-store reach travelled with the behaviour, not with the
    # filename: the namespace constant, the per-bucket store resolver and the
    # attachment store are the same three imports the pre-split module made.
    cadrumo.application.ledger.evidence -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.profile.buckets
    cadrumo.application.ledger.evidence -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.storage.attachment
    cadrumo.application.ledger.evidence -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.storage.envelope.secure_bound_repository
    cadrumo.application.ledger.evidence -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.storage.runtime_repository
    cadrumo.application.ledger.evidence -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.storage.secure_object_namespaces
    cadrumo.application.ledger.invoice_confirmation -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.profile.invoices
    cadrumo.application.ledger.invoice_confirmation -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.storage.attachment
    cadrumo.application.ledger.invoice_confirmation -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.storage.runtime_repository
    # Same shape as _confirmation_record above and _rule_repository below: a
    # SecureBoundRepository-backed ledger store naming its namespace constant
    # and its sensitivity class. Pinned rather than inverted for the same
    # reason they are -- the encrypted-store reach is the ambient design in
    # this package, and a lone port here would be the outlier. The module was
    # under an in-flight symbol rename when this pin landed, so the pin is
    # deliberately expressed at the module and target-package level, which no
    # symbol rename can invalidate.
    cadrumo.application.ledger.counterparty_establishment -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.storage.envelope.secure_bound_repository
    cadrumo.application.ledger.counterparty_establishment -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.storage.secure_object_namespaces
    # The read-time shape probe. DocumentShape is derived from a document's
    # own bytes by an INBOUND adapter, because reading an externally-authored
    # format is inbound-adapter work; the ledger consumes the answer.
    cadrumo.application.ledger.evidence_input -> cadrumo.adapters.inbound.einvoice.shape
    # The batch runner asks that same probe ONE question -- is this document
    # structured, and therefore readable without loading a model -- and consumes
    # the answer. Same shape and same justification as the pin above; it is the
    # probe's own caller, not a second reader. Its import is function-local, and
    # the contract sees it anyway (grimp walks function bodies), so the pin
    # records a reviewed decision rather than suppressing something that would
    # otherwise have gone unnoticed.
    cadrumo.application.ledger.batch_ingest -> cadrumo.adapters.inbound.einvoice.shape
    cadrumo.application.filing.history_repository -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.**
    cadrumo.application.filing.persistence_wiring -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.**
    cadrumo.application.inventory.service -> cadrumo.adapters.persistence.**
